<svelte:head>
	<title>Annihilation Atlas — Anindya Singh</title>
	<meta name="description" content="An anti-caste observatory of land, labour, classification, segregation, resistance, and memory." />
</svelte:head>

<main class="atlas-page">
	<header class="atlas-hero">
		<p class="eyebrow">Project zero · founding document</p>
		<h1>Annihilation<br />Atlas</h1>
		<p class="declaration">An anti-caste observatory of land, labour, classification, segregation, resistance, and memory.</p>
		<p class="intro">This project begins from a position, not a pretence of neutrality. Caste and jati are systems of material and social domination. They organise property, labour, intimacy, knowledge, space, and violence. The purpose of this atlas is to assist their annihilation—not to preserve hierarchy as heritage, nor to make oppressed people more legible to power.</p>
	</header>

	<section class="manifesto ruled">
		<p class="section-number">01 · Position</p>
		<h2>What we stand for</h2>
		<div class="principles">
			<p>Anti-caste thought is the project’s foundation. Marxian analysis gives us tools for studying land, labour, ownership, exploitation, and accumulation; it cannot be used to dissolve caste into class.</p>
			<p>Dalit, Bahujan, Adivasi, minority, feminist, and workers’ knowledge will be treated as constitutive of the archive—not commentary appended to official knowledge.</p>
			<p>Every map must ask who owns, who labours, who appropriates, who is excluded, and how those relations are reproduced and resisted.</p>
			<p>State records, surveys, censuses, and colonial texts are evidence about institutions and their categories. They are not transparent descriptions of social truth.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="refusals ruled">
		<p class="section-number">02 · Refusals</p>
		<h2>What this project will never become</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>A surname-to-caste identification machine.</li>
			<li>A searchable directory of individuals or vulnerable settlements.</li>
			<li>A ranking of jatis, communities, or supposedly fixed social traits.</li>
			<li>A caste-coloured map that disguises uncertain classifications as fact.</li>
			<li>A spectacle of atrocity in which oppressed people appear only as victims.</li>
			<li>A database that publishes sensitive material merely because it can.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="evidence ruled">
		<p class="section-number">03 · Evidence</p>
		<h2>No number without its history</h2>
		<p>Every published measure will remain attached to its source, date, geographic unit, category-maker, exclusions, uncertainty, and known contestations. “Not collected,” “not released,” “not comparable,” and “unsafe to publish” are valid findings.</p>
		<div class="record">
			<span>VALUE</span><span>SOURCE</span><span>PLACE + PERIOD</span><span>WHO CLASSIFIED?</span><span>WHAT IS ABSENT?</span><span>WHO CONTESTS IT?</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="prototype ruled">
		<div>
			<p class="section-number">04 · Prototype I</p>
			<h2>What the map cannot say</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="prototype-copy">
			<p>The first release will be deliberately bounded: one place and one material question, joined to a classification history, an archive of absence, and a history of resistance.</p>
			<ol>
				<li><strong>The Map</strong><span>A spatial claim that reveals its own limits.</span></li>
				<li><strong>The Measure</strong><span>Land, labour, occupation, housing, or education.</span></li>
				<li><strong>The File</strong><span>How an administrative category was produced and contested.</span></li>
				<li><strong>The Absence</strong><span>What the available evidence cannot tell us.</span></li>
				<li><strong>The Struggle</strong><span>Movements, organisation, refusal, and collective memory.</span></li>
			</ol>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="data-desk ruled">
		<p class="section-number">05 · Data desk</p>
		<h2>What we need first</h2>
		<p class="desk-intro">Collect original files together with questionnaires, codebooks, technical reports, and download dates. Do not clean or rename the originals. The first geographic case will determine which subset becomes Prototype I.</p>
		<div class="source-grid">
			<article><p class="priority">Start here</p><h3>Census 2011 SC/ST tables</h3><p>Population, work, occupation, education, housing, and district or sub-district identifiers. These establish broad administrative patterns, not jati-level truth.</p><a href="https://censusindia.gov.in/census.website/en/data/tables">Census of India tables ↗</a></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Start here</p><h3>Periodic Labour Force Survey</h3><p>Unit-level employment, occupation, industry, wages, social group, gender, and survey weights. We need the data files and their layouts for one annual round.</p><a href="https://microdata.gov.in/NADA/index.php/catalog/central">Government microdata catalogue ↗</a></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Material relations</p><h3>Agriculture Census</h3><p>Operational holdings and area by social group, gender, tenure, and holding size. Its measure of operational holdings must not be mistaken for complete ownership relations.</p><a href="https://agcensus.gov.in/">Agriculture Census ↗</a></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Institutions</p><h3>Crime in India</h3><p>District or state tables on recorded offences against Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, accompanied by definitions and reporting limitations.</p><a href="https://ncrb.gov.in/crime-in-india-year-wise.html">NCRB reports ↗</a></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Labour + abolition</p><h3>Sanitation work records</h3><p>Manual-scavenging surveys, NAMASTE documents, sewer and septic-tank death records, rehabilitation reports, and the survey instruments that produced official counts.</p><a href="https://socialjustice.gov.in/schemes/37/archive">Social Justice Ministry archive ↗</a></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Health + reproduction</p><h3>NFHS-5</h3><p>Household and individual microdata for health, education, assets, social group, religion, and gender. Access requires registration and careful disclosure control.</p><a href="https://dhsprogram.com/data/dataset/India_Standard-DHS_2020.cfm">DHS data access ↗</a></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Counter-archive</p><h3>Movement materials</h3><p>Anti-caste periodicals, pamphlets, speeches, oral histories, union records, community publications, memorials, and local-language scholarship. Begin with bibliographic metadata; copyright and consent govern publication.</p><span>Libraries, movement archives, personal collections</span></article>
			<article><p class="priority">Cartography</p><h3>Boundaries and concordances</h3><p>Village, sub-district, district, and state boundaries matched to the year of each dataset, plus a documented crosswalk for boundary changes.</p><a href="https://censusindia.gov.in/census.website/en/data/geospatial">Census geospatial resources ↗</a></article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="contribute ruled">
		<p class="section-number">06 · Immediate collection brief</p>
		<h2>Bring the archive in intact</h2>
		<div><p>For every source, preserve:</p><ul><li>Original downloaded file</li><li>Questionnaire and codebook</li><li>Methodology or sampling document</li><li>Source URL and access date</li><li>Licence or terms of use</li><li>Your notes about missing fields or access barriers</li></ul></div>
		<p class="closing">The next decision is political and empirical: the first place, and the first material relation we want to make visible.</p>
	</section>
</main>

